    "created_at": "2016-06-03T06:54:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-06-03T06:54:41Z",
    "body": "I think this makes sense. Using the construction stack, createElement works in a constructor being invoked by an upgrade reaction.\n\nThis might be slightly OT, but I believe ES6 prevents multiple calls to super. In that case, HTMLElement constructor step 4 could say \"If definition's construction stack is empty, or top of stack is an already constructed marker, ...\" This would make more things behave as you would expect, like using createElement or new in a constructor. Then step 6 can be deleted. If an author creates a new element before calling super, the eventual call to super would substitute a new element. But that would still be caught by \"upgrade an element\" step 6. We already have to handle return-a-different-object; this would make poaching from the construction stack just another instance of return-a-different-object and handle that in one place.\n\nI realize this stuff is complex so I'm probably missing something but might be worth your consideration.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
