    "body": "The construction stack exists only for the purpose of identifying the upgraded element. In the case of creating an element synchronously, there isn't an element to upgrade, and the construction stack is unnecessary.\n\nUnfortunately, construction stack imposes some restrictions on what a custom element's constructor can do.  In particular, the constructor of a custom element C1 cannot construct another instance of C1 until `super()` is called because `HTMLElement` constructor cannot identify which `super()` call corresponds to the element being upgraded.\n\nOn the other hand, [create an element](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-create-element) doesn't need to impose such a restriction because `HTMLElement`'s constructor does not need to identify the element to upgrade and simply creates a new instance.  This allows C1's constructor to construct another instance of C1 before invoking its own `super()`.  This design was chosen to make the semantics to [create an element](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-create-element) matches that of `new C1`.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
